`applyPatch(document, patch)` is now exported from `src/index.ts` and implements all six RFC 6902 operations. The full suite passes, including the new tests in `test/patch.test.ts`. Those tests cover the RFC's appendix A examples, a range of error cases, the no-mutation guarantee, and a `__proto__` key.

- **Atomic and non-mutating:** the document is deep-cloned first and the operations run on the clone. If any operation fails, the function throws and returns nothing.
- **Pointers:** it reuses the existing RFC 6901 token decoding. An array index must be plain digits with no leading zero, and `-` appends to an array. An index past the end of the array is an error.
- **Errors:** it throws for each of these:
  - a patch that isn't an array;
  - an operation that isn't an object, or has an unknown `op`;
  - a missing `path`, `value` or `from`;
  - a path that doesn't exist;
  - a failed `test`;
  - a `move` into one of its own children.
- **`test`:** it uses deep equality, so object key order doesn't matter.
- **Decisions the RFC leaves open:**
  - Removing the document root (`remove` with path `""`) throws.
  - A `move` to the same location does nothing.
  - A `__proto__` key is set as an ordinary own property, so a patch can't change `Object.prototype`.

I haven't committed anything.

`applyPatch(document, patch)` is now exported from `src/index.ts` and implements all six RFC 6902 operations. I added `test/patch.test.ts`, and `npm test` passes. That run covers the RFC's own examples for each operation, the failure cases and the no-mutation check.

- **No mutation:** it works on a deep clone of the document and clones every `value` it inserts. The input document and patch are never changed, and nothing in the result is shared with them. Because the whole patch is applied to the clone, a failing patch gives no partial result.
- **Errors:** it throws when:
  - the patch isn't an array;
  - an operation isn't an object;
  - `op` is unknown;
  - `path` or `from` is missing or isn't a valid pointer;
  - `value` is missing where it's required;
  - a target or source doesn't exist, or an array index is out of range;
  - a `test` fails;
  - a `move` targets a child of its own source.
- **Array indexes:** `-` and an index equal to the length are accepted only for `add`. Indexes with leading zeros are rejected.
- **Root path:** `add` and `replace` at the root path `""` replace the whole document. `remove` at `""` throws.
- **`test`:** it compares by JSON value, so object key order doesn't matter and array order does.
- **`__proto__`:** a member named `__proto__` is stored as an ordinary property instead of changing the object's prototype.

The pointer parsing reuses the existing `resolvePointer` and its escape handling in the same file. I did not commit.

I found two ways `applyPatch` accepted patches that RFC 6902 treats as invalid, and fixed both in `src/index.ts`. The 30 tests pass, including a new one for these two cases that failed before the fix.

- **Sparse patch arrays:** a patch like `[ , {...}]` had its empty slots skipped without error. Every slot is now checked, and an empty one throws as an invalid operation.
- **`undefined` values:** `{op:"add", path:"/b", value: undefined}` was accepted and produced a member that disappears when serialised. A `value` of `undefined` now counts as missing and throws. `null` is still a valid value.

I probed about 25 other edge cases and they already behaved as the RFC requires. These covered:
- root paths and non-canonical array indexes (`/00`, `/1e0`);
- `-` and out-of-range indexes;
- `move` into its own child and `move` onto itself;
- `copy`, `test` and `replace` on missing targets;
- `__proto__` handling;
- atomic failure.

I recorded the work as `task-003` and `dl-003`, both submitted and now waiting for approval. The change is committed.

I didn't touch the earlier `dl-002` or `task-002`; they are still pending approval from before this session.

I added `createPatch(from, to)` to `src/index.ts`. It returns an RFC 6902 patch that `applyPatch` turns into `to`, and it doesn't mutate either input. The full suite passes, including the new tests in `test/create-patch.test.ts`. They cover:
- a small change in a nested document producing a single operation;
- adds, removes, and replaces on objects and arrays;
- `~` and `/` escaping in keys, plus `null` values and `__proto__` keys;
- non-mutation, and no shared structure between the patch and the inputs;
- round trips through `applyPatch` for every pair of sample documents.

How it works:
- **Objects:** it compares them member by member and emits only the differences. Equal documents give an empty patch.
- **Scalars and type changes:** a changed scalar, or a value whose type changed, becomes a `replace` at its own path. If the root itself changes type, that's a `replace` at `""`.
- **Arrays:** it skips the unchanged start and end, recurses into elements at shared positions, then removes or adds the leftover elements. A single insert, removal, append, or edit gives one operation.
- **Operations used:** only `add`, `remove`, and `replace` are produced.

The patch isn't guaranteed to be the smallest possible. If the middle of an array is shifted or reordered, you get several replaces, adds, and removes instead of a `move`.

I recorded the work as `task-005` and the design as `dl-005-createpatch-minimal-diff-semantics`, and submitted both. They are waiting for approval, which I haven't given. The code is committed.

`resolvePointer(document, pointer)` is now exported from `src/index.ts` and implements RFC 6901. All 36 tests pass with `npm test`, which includes every example from section 5 of the RFC. I didn't run `tsc` or a linter, since the repo has neither installed.

- **Invalid pointers:** a non-empty pointer that doesn't start with `/`, or one with a bad `~` escape such as `/a~` or `/a~2`, throws a `SyntaxError`. A non-string pointer throws a `TypeError`.
- **Pointers that don't resolve:** throws a `RangeError`. That covers:
  - a missing member;
  - an out-of-range array index;
  - an index with a leading zero or sign, such as `01` or `+1`;
  - the `-` token, which names no existing element;
  - a step into a primitive or `null`.
- **Inherited properties:** only an object's own members count. `/constructor`, `/toString` and `/length` don't resolve, and a real `__proto__` key from `JSON.parse` does.
- **Unescaping:** `~1` is decoded before `~0`, so `~01` gives `~1` rather than `/`.

The tests are in `test/pointer.test.ts`. I added a short section to `README.md` documenting the function and its errors, and committed everything as one commit.

`PROJECT_RULES.md` asks for a doc comment on every exported API element, which I added. It also asks for a linter and a declared coverage threshold, and the repo has neither yet.

I reviewed how `applyPatch` checks and applies patches and found three cases where it accepted patches the RFC treats as invalid. They're fixed and committed (`a77ea85`), and all 142 tests pass. I couldn't run a type check because TypeScript isn't installed in the repo; Node runs the sources by stripping the types and found no problems.

**Fixed:**
1. **Gaps in the patch list were skipped.** In a patch like `[ , {op:"remove",…}]` the empty slot was ignored and the rest still ran. A gap is now rejected as an operation that is not an object, with its position in the error.
2. **Arrays with gaps inside a `value` were accepted**, and the gaps were copied into the result, which then isn't valid JSON. They are now rejected with `InvalidPatchError`.
3. **A `value` that contains itself crashed** with a stack-overflow `RangeError` instead of a patch error. It is now rejected with `InvalidPatchError`. A value that holds the same object twice, without a loop, is still accepted.

`value` is now also read only once. Each fix started as a failing test, plus one test for the "same object twice" case. The README and ADR 0002 (now version 2) record the rule.

**Already correct:** I checked the RFC's appendix examples and the edge cases around them, and these all follow the standard:
- `-` at the end of an array path, and indexes up to the array's length;
- leading-zero indexes;
- `move` to the same location or into the parent;
- `move` and `copy` of the whole document;
- extra members being ignored;
- test comparisons by number value and regardless of member order;
- a failing patch leaving the input unchanged.

**Left as they are:** the RFC leaves these open and ADR 0002 already records the current choices. Any of them could be what users mean by failures not behaving the way they read the standard, so tell me if you want one changed:
- **The whole patch is checked before anything runs.** In `[remove /missing, {op:"bogus"}]`, operation 1 is reported as an `InvalidPatchError`, even though applying the operations in order would fail first at operation 0 with a conflict.
- **A `test` whose path doesn't exist** raises `PatchConflictError`, not `PatchTestFailedError`.
- **`remove` of the whole document (`""`)** is treated as a conflict.
- **Duplicate members such as two `op`s** (RFC example A.13) can't be detected once the patch has been parsed with `JSON.parse`.

The document itself isn't checked for being valid JSON either; that isn't part of RFC 6902.

Implemented `importTasks(list, text)` and exported it from [src/index.ts](/workspace/src/index.ts:40).

It:
- imports valid `title;priority;tags` lines;
- defaults empty priority to `normal`;
- skips blank lines;
- returns imported count plus one-based rejected line details;
- preserves existing validation and duplicate-title rules.

Added tests and updated [README.md](/workspace/README.md:12). `npm test` passes.
